.\" bmb_mcp(1). make turns bmb_mcp.1.in into bmb_mcp.1, then into the
.\" site's bmb_mcp.html; edit the .in. man/test_man_*.sh check it
.\" against the programs: see doc/dev/documentation.md.
.TH BMB_MCP 1 2026-11-01 "blas\-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation, no justification: both hurt in a terminal.
.nh
.ds AD l
.nr HY 0
.ad l
.SH NAME
bmb_mcp \- an MCP server over blas\-microbenchmark, for AI agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmb_mcp
.RB [ \-\-results\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-time\-cap
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-\-memory\-limit
.IR bytes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bmb_mcp
lets an AI agent use the benchmarks of
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1):
list the routines, describe the machine, run a benchmark or the suite,
read a result and make the report.
It is a Model Context Protocol server: the agent's client starts it and
talks to it on its standard input and output, a JSON\-RPC 2.0 message a
line, and it is not run by hand.
.PP
A tool's arguments are fields, never a command line: a routine, its
sizes and threads, its arguments
.RB ( trans ,
.BR uplo ,
\&...), the run's options.
Every run is checked before it starts, and refused with a message that
says what to do instead (see
.BR LIMITS );
every result is verified against a reference, as the benchmarks do by
default.
.PP
.B bmb_mcp
finds the benchmarks as
.BR bmb_suite (1)
does: those of the build tree it is in, or those installed with it, in
.IR @pkglibexecdir@ .
It needs Python 3, and nothing else.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-results\-dir " dir"
Where the results and the reports go, created if need be, and the only
place a tool reads from or writes to.
Default:
.IR ./bmb\-results .
.TP
.BI \-\-time\-cap " seconds"
The longest a run may take: one that goes past is stopped, and the tool
says so.
Default: 900.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory\-limit " bytes"
The most memory a run's operands may take.
Default: a quarter of the memory the system says is available.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the usage.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Show the version.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B list_routines
The routines, filtered by level, precision or family: each one's level,
precision, family, and what
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-m ,
.B \-M
and
.B \-K
are for it.
.TP
.B describe_machine
What
.B \-\-version
says: the BLAS library and its version, the kernels it chose, the CPU,
caches, frequency and OS; with the CPUs this process may use, the memory
limit, the results directory and the time cap.
.TP
.B run_benchmark
One routine's benchmark, with its sizes
.RB ( vector_size ,
.BR dim1 ,
.BR dim2 ,
.BR dim3 ),
.BR threads ,
the routine's arguments, and the run's options
.RB ( layout ,
.BR cache ,
.BR iterations ,
.BR min_time ,
.BR label ,
.BR peak_gflops ,
\&...), each named as the long option it stands for.
The result goes into the results directory as JSON, named
.IR routine \- n .json
or as
.BR name ;
the tool returns a row a point (threads, sizes, time, median, IQR,
GFLOP/s, GB/s), whether it was verified, and the warnings.
.TP
.B run_suite
.BR bmb_suite (1)
into a directory of the results directory: every routine, or some
levels, precisions or routines, at a profile's sizes, for one library
or several
.RB ( blas ,
.BR flexiblas ).
It returns a line a result, with its best rate, and the benchmarks that
failed.
.TP
.B read_result
A result file of the results directory, summarised as
.B run_benchmark
does.
.TP
.B make_report
.BR bmb_report (1)
on a directory of the results directory, a peak given or not; it
returns the page's path.
.SH LIMITS
Each is checked before anything runs.
.IP \(bu 2
A run's arguments are those the benchmark takes: they are first given
to it with
.BR BMB_COUNTS ,
which checks them and counts each point's operands without measuring or
allocating anything.
.IP \(bu
The largest point's operands fit within the memory limit.
.IP \(bu
No thread count is above the number of CPUs the process may use.
.IP \(bu
Every path, and every name given, is inside the results directory, and
a result already there is not overwritten.
.PP
.BR run_suite 's
.B blas
names suites by their paths, and runs their benchmarks: they are
programs, which
.BR bmb_suite (1)
reads the backend of by running them.
Configure the client to ask before it calls
.BR run_suite ,
or give it only paths of installs you trust.
.PP
A run that goes past the time cap is stopped.
A benchmark that fails, a wrong result included, is the tool's error,
with the benchmark's message: the server goes on.
.SH EXAMPLES
A client that reads its servers from JSON, such as Claude Code's
.IR .mcp.json ,
starts it with:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{"mcpServers": {"blas\-microbenchmark":
    {"command": "bmb_mcp", "args": ["\-\-results\-dir", "bmb\-results"]}}}
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the client closes its end; 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1),
.BR bmb_suite (1),
.BR bmb_report (1)
